			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a19" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts36" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts37">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Okay.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a21" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts40" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts41">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I was born November 7th, 1950, right here in Palatka. And it was at the Lawson San Sanitorium is what they called it back in the day. But it was a black hospital.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a23" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts44" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts45">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well, I have three, I mean, there are three girls, three, I have two sisters, one older, one younger. My mother is actually from Alachua County. Oh, awesome. On, uh, well, going up Highway 20, you will come to a little, I call it a settlement, but a little community. Rochelle, it's on the left,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a25" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts48" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts49">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And, uh, she was one of 10 children. Wow. And the only one that graduated from high school. And then her dad, uh, 'cause he sent her down to Daytona to quote further her education. 'cause she started school in Rochelle. They had a one room schoolhouse, but they had relatives down in Daytona. And, uh, he sent her there so she could finish high school. And then she went on to college.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a27" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts52" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts53">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well, yes, some of them did. You know, because back in the day, I mean, my mother was born in 1915 &lt;laugh&gt;. So back in the day, there was not a lot, lot of opportunities. Yeah. You know, then, and you know, we think, you know, in the fifties, sixties, that's a long time ago. But when you're looking back to 1915, it's, it's, it was much more difficult. And segregation was a whole lot more pronounced. Oh, yeah. Then, and then my, uh, father, uh, is a native, uh, Putnam County, and he was, uh, born in 1902. Wow. Most people can't believe that. 'cause when I was in high school, the kids used to tease me saying, your daddy could be your granddaddy. &lt;laugh&gt;. Yes. He could have been, but he didn't. I mean, he isn't, he wasn't rather, but, uh, he now he only completed eighth grade. Oh, okay.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a28" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts54" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts55">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Because back then, actually when you finished eighth grade, you were eligible to teach. Wow. Yes. And so that's why I tell people a lot of what we think is just totally antique or ancient is not that far ago. Yeah. But I do remember that because I learned that, you know, just by growing up here and knowing teachers. And then when I found out that they only had an eighth grade education, that is interesting. But then as time moved on, and, uh, those teachers were grandfathered in, but highly encouraged to go on and continue, they didn't have to go back to high school, but they had to go on and, uh, enroll in college to get at least a bachelor's degree. Oh, okay. But my mom, she had done it all before then, because when, uh, she graduated from high school and college, things were a little bit better then.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a29" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts56" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts57">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But with my dad, he had to help the family, you know, because being poor and living, you know, in the deep south, a lot of them, and even some whites, you know, I'm sure was in the same situation if you were not wealthy. Yeah. And so he, um, he went to work just to bring in some income, you know, for the family. What did he work as? He did farm work initially, but he eventually, uh, worked in, uh, he actually, when he retired, I remember this because my dad retired before I graduated from high school. &lt;laugh&gt;. Wow. He was 65. And he in the context, right? It does. And he, um, he was one of the maintenance workers. They had two in maintenance way back in the day. And they had to have a black and a white, because the schools were still seg segregated. And he, and this white guy, they became very good friends. And, and the most interesting thing was they were the two maintenance guys, even though they worked together on their own, you know, they didn't go through any legal things to, to do it through the school district, but they became very good friends. And so the family became two big families, and they were maintenance, and both their wives were home economics teachers, &lt;laugh&gt;.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a31" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts60" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts61">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Interesting. It was the most interesting. That is very coincidental. And we celebrated birthdays, everything together.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a33" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts64" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts65">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes. He was a white guy. Yes. Okay.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a35" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts68" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts69">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And it just turned out to be that way. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a37" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts72" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts73">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>That Right. 'cause I remember my dad getting up early in the morning, especially in the winter time, because back then schools were not air conditioned. But on cold winter mornings, you know, they'd call each other, say, okay, we're gonna meet at, like I said, 'cause they had their own little thing. We're gonna meet at, let's say, Mosley Elementary. And they would have to literally go to every school before school started to light the, it wasn't the furnace then. Well, whatever it was. But it's like lighting the furnace so that the school Wow. So that the schools could, could, uh, generate some heat.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a39" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts76" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts77">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>County. And when I became &lt;laugh&gt;, an administrator was just so funny. I wanted to re refurbish a room in the school and convert it from an old indoor PE for inclement weather to my curriculum resource teacher's office, &lt;laugh&gt; and maintenance. Oh. They wanted to give me a hard time. And I said, listen. I said, I know what you can do, and probably a little bit of what you can't do, but this is possible. I said, because back in the day, my dad was one of two. Yeah. And he went and they did it all down and put cold. They did it for the whole, from Crescent City to, what is that? Johnson Crossroads. Wow. They, and so they had to work the whole county. They</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a40" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts78" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts79">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>All. And I, I said, if you, if you do it for me, I will make sure that we have breakfast for you and snacks &lt;laugh&gt;. And what they say, I don't get them doing it. If you feed them, they'll come. And so I had my cafeteria staff to make sure that they cooked special breakfast, and the ladies at the school, they would bring in different kind of snacks and whatever. I mean, hell, and they, Hey, they were there for, I would do for about a month and a half. Wow. But they did it. And she was, because the curriculum resource teacher, I was principal then at James, a long elementary, and the curriculum resource teacher, she said, miss HI just thought you liked me, but I don't, I'm not sure now. 'cause it smelled like an old gym &lt;laugh&gt; locker room, believe it or not. It was bad.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a42" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts82" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts83">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes. But it turned out to be the best resource room in the county. Wow. Wow. Because, you know, they, they did it up nice. But it was, you know, you feed them, they'll come and they worked hard, and</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a44" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts86" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts87">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>&lt;laugh&gt;. Right, right. But yeah. And, and we traveled a lot in my family. Oh, cool. Because I remember at three years old, my, my mother had a sister living in Philadelphia. And back then, it wasn't as dangerous for people to travel other than with blacks. They couldn't stay at a hotel, but we could ride the train. And so when school was out, my mother would just send us to Philadelphia. So I knew Philadelphia, like I knew Palatka. Wow. Because that's where we spent our summers. That's awesome. And so it was three girls getting on the train. And I remember I was only three at the time when we started, and we knew just about every conductor on the East Coast Railroad. And I just met Smith last Saturday, which is, I was just, I said that was the best way I could end Black History Month was being in Crescent City at the, a Philip Randolph Memorial Highway. They just dedicated down there. And one of the, one of a Philip Randolph's coworkers was there. Wow. He is 103 years young. And I'm telling you, the man's mind is so sharp because I, I was one of the MCs helping with the program, but afterwards I got to actually meet him. He doesn't get around. Well, you know, because of his legs. And he told me it was, he had an ingrown toenail. I said, well, Mr. Sally, we can fix that &lt;laugh&gt;. He said, but I do forget some things. I said, we all do &lt;laugh&gt;.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a45" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts88" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts89">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Because, you know, he didn't have, and he didn't have to think about his responses. That was just amazing. Because University of Florida was there through this pro, through this project. They were there videoing. And it was an older white lady was doing the, she was the videographer.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a47" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts92" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts93">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes. Because he ended up sitting at the table where I was sitting, because after the program, the organizer of that event that day, and the president of they Phillip Randolph, uh, organization in Putnam County, she had a luncheon on the lawn afterwards at her house. And so we ended up sitting there, and I just told him, I said, I'm, I said, this is the best thing that could have happened to me to just know that I met someone that old, that sharp. And it was just a pleasure. That was, I mean, never would've ever imagined that that would ever happen. People</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a49" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts96" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts97">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>They were right. 'cause he said he was born in 1918, and he remembered he could tell me things when he was a, a toddler.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a51" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts100" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts101">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I was a toddler. And I was telling him the story. Me and my sisters catching the train and riding to Philadelphia. And he says, you know, then I probably saw you back in the day &lt;laugh&gt;. He said, because he worked the, the East Coast Railroad. And I was just amazed.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a53" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts104" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts105">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>My, uh, when I graduated from Bethune Cookman with my bachelor's, my youngest and older sister, they both graduated from Florida a and m. And so did my mother</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a55" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts108" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts109">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So the four of them graduated from Florida and m but I graduated, got my bachelor's from Bethune Cookman. And Miss Bethune was actually my mother's godmother. Wow. When her father take, took her to Daytona, and, you know, so she didn't, she wasn't one of the five girls that started the school, but she was very close with Ms. Bethune and going to her house. That's amazing. You know, in the evenings and on the weekends and things of that nature. Wow.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a57" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts112" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts113">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Actually, uh, I said the first college that accepted me, I was going as long as it wasn't St. John's &lt;laugh&gt;, because I did not want to stay in Palatka.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a59" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts116" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts117">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. And actually, I, I really wanted to attend, for whatever reason, I can't even tell you now, but I remember Prairie View a and m in Texas. I thought I can go a long ways off and I can be an adult because I was only 16. Oh, wow. So, you know, it was like, get away from family, you know, get away from your mama and your daddy and just be an adult.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a61" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts120" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts121">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But I mean, I did, you know, I really did enjoy it. And I liked the size of the school because you really got to know your professors. Yeah. And part of their agreement to work there is that they had to, um, work with any student that felt like they needed extra help. So they had to, you know, be willing to tutor students. That's amazing. You know, after hours in order to help them along, because it was just, at that time, schools were still segregated. You know, they were getting a little bit better, but, you know. Was</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a63" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts124" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts125">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes, it was co-ed. When Ms. Bethune first started it, it was girls and then Mr. Bethune, well, it was Mary. Yeah. Mary McLeod. And then she met Mr. Bethune. And, you know, they got married the whole bit, but &lt;laugh&gt; &lt;laugh&gt;, she had the girls school and he had a boys school. And they just came together. Yeah, they came together. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a65" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts128" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts129">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>From Florida a and m. Yes. And they both, well, my younger sister has her master's. And it's interesting because I did tell you though, that they were all four. I mean, the three of them are all home economics majors. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. Wow. &lt;laugh&gt;. But my mother got her master's from Temple.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a67" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts132" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts133">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And, uh, my older sister doesn't have her master's, but she was very successful. Well, she started teaching and she didn't really care for that. Mm. She says too much work for such a little pay and the hours, you know, I mean, yeah. That just wasn't what she wanted to do. But she did teach for three or four years at St. Augustine High School. Oh,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a69" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts136" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts137">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And my younger sister, she, it was a career for her because she So she's a teacher? Yes. She, she's, well, she's, we all retired now. Oh, okay. Oh, yeah. So she, uh, she retired in Bely 'cause she's down in Palm Beach County. Oh,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a71" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts140" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts141">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Or? No, actually, my older sister has moved. She moved back several years ago now. Uh, just prior to my mother's passing. And, uh, because I was the only one here for many, many years. And, uh, she moved back and she's retired. She, she left the teaching career and she went into, uh, insurance.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a73" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts144" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts145">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so she did insurance in Florida as well as Louisiana.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a75" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts148" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts149">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well, we, we were very close knit. We are very close knit now. We talk, you know, even though she's down in Bell Glee, we talk almost every day. Sometimes more than once a day. It's more than I can</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a77" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts152" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts153">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And, uh, and we travel a lot together. We've been all around the world. That's awesome. You know, traveling. And like I said, my mother, she loved to travel and she did a lot of that, you know, even on her own. And, uh, so she, she instilled in us, she, her, one of her mantras was, if, if you're gonna grow, you have to go, you have to see more than life is not just Putnam County. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. Yeah. And so she wanted us to just, you know, spread our wings. My dad wasn't too cool about it initially, especially with me. 'cause my mom asked, well, what you want for graduation from high school? I said, if I could get the car for a couple of weeks, I would a road trip. I don't trip need. Right. And that's exactly what I did. That's awesome. My dad had a fit about it. But my mom convinced him. She said, well, she's graduated from high school. Yes. She's 16. And if what we have taught her, you know, we, we have to trust that what we, how we raised her and what we taught her, you know, she'll be able to live by it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a79" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts156" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts157">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I left here. I went to Tallahassee because my sister was in my younger sister. No, wait a minute. When I, okay. I did. I went to tall Tallahassee and I remember leaving there. Went up into Atlanta, visited a few friends up there, and then went all the way up to New York. &lt;laugh&gt;.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a81" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts160" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts161">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I had a doll with me. My, my younger sister had this doll, we call it Judy, to today. She still has this doll, believe it or not, still, this doll stands about three feet tall.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a83" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts164" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts165">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>It was one of these that you could almost hold a hand and walk, try to walk with it. And I took one of my father's, uh, hats. Not a cat, but a hat, and put it on the dog.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a84" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts166" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts167">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right on the God. Right in the passenger seat. And let me tell you, Judy and I, we just travel</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a86" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts170" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts171">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And you know, they told me, they said, you must stop, you must gas up once you, if the Yeah, once the needle got down to a quarter of a tank, you must buy gas so you don't run outta gas. Uh, and you need to make sure that you find a good place to stay, you know, hotel or whatever, you know. Uh, on my road trip, and then I was &lt;laugh&gt;. I was thinking on my own. And the funny thing was, I thought, why should I pay money for a, a hotel that'd be more money than I can spend. Oh</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a87" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts172" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts173">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So I slept at all truck stops. &lt;laugh&gt;,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a89" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts176" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts177">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I said, there's a lot of people at the truck stop. And I would leave a little bit of, you know, room open my, open my window so I could have some air coming in. And Judy and I, we slept on at road stops, at truck stops, rather. You</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a91" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts180" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts181">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I wanted to see the world.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a93" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts184" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts185">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>In your Oh yes. Be able to become independent. Yeah. My mother, that's what she told my dad. She says, she said, she told him that, uh, you know, we have to be independent because if, and I don't know, she used to always tell us this story about if something ever happened to me and or your dad and somebody has to adopt you. And it may be, and she always used Chinese for whatever reason. It could be someone from China or Africa somewhere. You gotta learn how to adapt. Adapt. Yeah. And so that's how she taught us to eat a lot of different food. You know, try different things. Uh, don't really meet strangers. You know, you look at that person as a person that's gonna bring something positive into your life. So it just works for me.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a95" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts188" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts189">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so I just had fun. I just, our family, you know, and she tried to make sure that we went to, during that time, you know, all the, the, the, the, uh, amusement parks and things like that all over the state. She said, you need to know your own state. And then you branch out,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a97" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts192" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts193">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. Yeah. And that's what I've done.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a99" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts196" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts197">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Here? The community was, well, I could, you know, we know it was, it was segregated. But, um, the people that I came in contact with were, you know, they were pretty nice people, you know. Uh, even though we live, 'cause I live out in San Mateo. Yeah. Do you know where that is? Right. Uh,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a101" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts200" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts201">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Lives there. Yes. Because she lives, right. She lives mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. Not too far. A few blocks over from me. So I lived, I've been there all my life. I've traveled all over, but I've always lived in San Mateo. And, uh, I went to school here in Palatka. I didn't go to East Palka like a lot of the black students did, because my mother was an educator. Both parents were working and it was very difficult for them to find babysitters. And so she found a private certified teacher who had her own school. Uh, I'll never forget, we called it Fox Kindergarten, but F-A-U-L-K-S, Fox Kindergarten. Anyway, she taught from, now we call it pre-kindergarten, but it was preschool through third grade.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a103" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts204" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts205">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So you And so being and starting there at three years old. Yeah. Because she couldn't find a babysitter.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a105" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts208" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts209">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. It wasn't daycare at all, believe me. &lt;laugh&gt;, I never learned to do manuscript until, until I got to college, taking my Wow. Uh, elementary ed classes. Because she started you out writing your name. &lt;laugh&gt;</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a107" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts212" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts213">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And we had geography. We had everything. Wow. As it was nothing. It was so far advanced, more advanced than, uh, the regular public school kids. And so when I finished third grade there, I was seven mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. And then my mom had to go before the school board because they, they said I was too young to be in fourth grade. That's what second graders, you know, that's where, where second graders were. So she said, and I guess I get a lot of that from her. She said, well then test her and place her wherever she belongs. &lt;laugh&gt;. And then they said after the test, well, we're gonna place her in fourth grade. And then when it was time, well, central Academy was grades seven through 12. I went to Central Academy Elementary from grades four through six. And then Central Academy High School was seven through 12.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a108" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts214" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts215">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Okay. So when I graduated from Central Academy Elementary, going to the high school, and my mom was teacher at the high school, then they didn't, they said I was too young to go to the high school because I was only 10. Wow. And it's like, that is young. Okay. So what does she do? You know, does she stay back another year because that's not gonna help. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. So they tested me, and then they didn't wanna put me in the grade again. 'cause I was usually a couple of years, you know, not when I think about all of this. And a couple of years more advanced than, you know, my peers. And there were, and it was a small private school because most of the students in that school, we went all the way through together. And I wanna say when I graduated, there were five of us the same age. Wow. And you know, so, but when I, when it was time to go to the high school, they agreed, the school board said if she goes, because they could not imagine a 10-year-old in a school with 18-year-old boys. And so they made me, and I was fortunate enough to be in my mother's homeroom. If you can imagine, &lt;laugh&gt; &lt;laugh&gt; from seventh through 12th grade. Wow.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a110" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts218" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts219">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And she always told us, she says, when I go to work, I'm at work and I'm not mom. So we had to call her Miss Asia. Miss</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a112" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts222" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts223">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I said, daddy, what's wrong with her? &lt;laugh&gt;? We can't call her mama at school &lt;laugh&gt;. He said, well, she's at work, so you gotta do what she says. I said, okay.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a114" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts226" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts227">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a115" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts228" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts229">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And seeing, I was brought up during the age 'cause when I was, and it tells a story about, you know, how long ago I was in school. Because when I became a principal, and I remember one of the pre-K and kindergarten teachers, I came in the classroom one day and they wanted to introduce me as Miss Cynthia. 'cause that's the way the kids were. I told 'em, I said, no, I'm, I don't, I don't do that. I said, in my community, in black community, it's always Mr. Or Miss whomever. I said, and his, and, and the same thing, there was this older gentleman that came to the school and he asked for Professor Asia. And that's a sign of respect in the black community was really big back then, because if you were a principal, you were the head. And so that, and it represented, uh, academics. So that meant that you were professor. I never called my principal Mr. Brooks. It was always Professor Brooks. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a117" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts232" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts233">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And I had this class one on one on black history, one morning &lt;laugh&gt; in my front office. I said, don't be afraid. I said, just take it as what it was. I said, 'cause every culture has their own differences, but we are more alike than we are different. I said, but that's one thing. I said, respect is very, very, uh, high on our, you know, priority list. Yeah,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a119" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts236" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts237">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh, yes. I said, you, you, you respect people. It doesn't matter whether you know them or not. Whether, you know, that's the town drunk or the preacher. You respected adults. Yeah. As adults.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a121" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts240" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts241">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>It started the year before I graduated. So I was a junior and they started trying to play around with it a little bit back in 66. And see, I graduated in 67.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a123" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts244" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts245">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>A little bit, but not a lot. But after I left, because I used to get phone calls all the time when I was in college, or while I would call home and, you know, I would hear all of these crazy things that were happening. And being that my mother was a teacher, I do remember when they did, you know, uh, began to move the, the faculty and staff around the teachers that went to Central, the white teachers that went over to Central Academy were given higher pay because their lives were being endangered. Oh. And then the district, for whatever reason, they put it in writing. And one of those letters was found by another faculty member. And that did not go over well. Yeah. Because it, before night, every teacher in Putnam County, every black teacher in Putnam County knew that they were not being paid more to go to the white community and teach. They were, but the whites were being paid more to come to the black schools to teach.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a125" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts248" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts249">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And it, but it happened. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a127" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts252" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts253">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And I know that my mother, she had ne she's a, she was, when she came to Putnam County, she was what they call then a home demonstration agent, which now is home. I mean, is extension agents through the Ag Center. And, uh, they had, she was, she was the black, and there was a white young lady, because, you know, they had to go out into the homes and teach people how to, you know, uh, keep house, you know, their bills, how to make sure they're paying their bill. So like ec Right. It's like home ec. But it was one-on-one in Okay. Into the homes. Oh, cool. And, uh, one of the things that I know that she ended up doing, uh, when she came and she wasn't familiar with the school district because she wasn't from here, and she did not realize that the blacks could not drive, could not ride the school bus. But that was in the forties. In the mid forties. And so she &lt;laugh&gt; she put in for requisition to have, uh, to to use a bus to take the kids on, uh, a field trip.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a129" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts256" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts257">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And she was told emphatically no. And they used the N word, don't ride the buses. And so, being my mother, she got in her car and she drove to Tallahassee and, uh, told them that she couldn't be effective doing her job if she was not allowed the resources that the whites were getting. Yeah. And like I tell people that, to me, that was the beginning of, uh, integrating the buses in Putnam County. But that was back in the forties. Because even this, the students from, uh, San Mateo, if they got to the high school, once they became high school, they had to walk to Palatka to school.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a131" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts260" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts261">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I can't even imagine that. Yeah. And I've done it before, just when I was a little bit younger and a little bit more energetic, a couple of friends than I, we would walk from our houses in San Mateo to seventh Street because that's where my church was. And one of the girls that were, her mother lived on seventh Street. And we would walk once a week from San Mateo to Palatka and then back.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a133" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts264" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts265">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>&lt;laugh&gt;. It is. But we got in our steps, like they do now, what they want, the 10,000 steps and all of that. But we got our steps in. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a135" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts268" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts269">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Loved it. Loved it. The teachers were very caring and nurturing and wanted us to succeed. They, their, their, their expectation of us was very high. And because they knew what they had gone through and they know that opportunities were slowly opening up. And even though you didn't have a whole lot of money, you could still go to school if you applied yourself. And so they were very, very instrumental in helping students to be prepared to go to college, as well as helping them to even get scholarships, you know? Um, because what I observed, even when I became a guidance counselor, 'cause I was a high school guidance counselor at one point, some of the scholarships were, uh, some of them had never been given to black students. And then some of them, um, were not always available, even though you may have heard about it, but they, you know, the guidance counselors, you know, uh, would advertise but not really recruit a lot of the students.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a136" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts270" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts271">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so, you know, to me, I enjoyed it because number one, my, I knew a lot of them since my mother was an educator. And, uh, the course offerings were good. Not the same as always, but what I do remember, which is, is sad, but it was true. We would get our textbooks from the discards and they were stamped, discarded. And they would have probably 10 or 12 kids will have had that same textbook. And when they would get rid of those books, those were the textbooks that we would get at Central Academy. And the kids in the white schools would get the new textbooks. And my mom and a lot of the teachers said, well, just think about it. You're getting the trash, but you're gonna make something of the trash because not everybody's, you know what the old saying is, some people's trash is another person's treasure. And that's the way that they present it to, to us. So that we wouldn't feel so bad about it. But we normally spent those first few days of school every year taping the books back together. Wow. So the librarian, you know, would order this, what they call the book tape. And we would put our books back together. We went on and we had lawyers, doctors, you name it. Every profession you can imagine. So</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a138" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts274" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts275">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh yes. Lots of them did. Some of 'em went on to the NFL because we had one of the, the best football teams in the state until we actually had to start playing schools outta Georgia. Wow. And I remember going to football games where, you know, central Academy would, I've never, we went and I can't remember the school, but I remember the score was 98 to nothing. And that was a football game. &lt;laugh&gt; not a basketball game, but a football game. That is</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a139" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts276" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts277">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. But I mean, but they were really, really good. So they got recruited a lot. So, uh, some of the kids that probably would've never been able to go to college, and we still have some of that today, but not as much. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. You know, they got football scholarships to college and then they went on to play and some of 'em went on to the NFL. That's amazing. But, you know, it was, it was just interesting. And I'll never forget the first scoreboard, the first electric scoreboard that we had for Central Academy. My father, because he worked in maintenance and he said he saw, he kept seeing this scoreboard. It was just in the warehouse. Yeah, it was in the warehouse. And he noticed that they never decided to put it anywhere. So he started asking about it and found that, he was told that that's, that they don't put electric scoreboards in the black schools.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a140" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts278" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts279">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so someone had to climb up, you know, and write, erase the school, whatever, you know, do the scoring manually. Yeah. And so one day he challenged them, he said, if you give me that scoreboard, I will get it up. And they eventually gave in, gave it to him, but they wouldn't give him any help to get it. 'cause by this time they had a few more in the maintenance department. And so he got some of his friends as well as some of the guys in the community. And they went out there. 'cause I remember it took him a week to put it up because they had no, no help. That's quick. Either a week, it was one or two weeks. Because I remember going home after school, my mom would cook dinner and bring it back to town and we'd sit there and shine the car light so that they could see how to, you know, install it and get the electrics up and everything. And from that point on, my dad never had to pay for a ticket, you know, to go to the games. And he, and he got sidelined. Seats. Seats. Oh, I missed the least they could do. Right. He got sideline seat play every game from then on. So</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a142" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts282" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts283">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And yes, they were very big. And, and he loved my dad. Like I said, he didn't graduate from high school or anything, but he loved sports himself. And so, uh, it was just, he loved kids and he knew how important education was. And he just thought that was a little thing that he could do for the, for the kids and just help him, help him out. You know, he got somebody climbing up on a ladder every time you gotta change the score. Yeah. &lt;laugh&gt; made no sense. But the school board was definitely not willing at that time. You know, the district, I'll put it, not necessarily the school board, but they were just not willing to just say, okay, we have this school board who needs it. That wasn't happening. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a144" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts286" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts287">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Lots of clubs. And one of the, you know, like I remember my math teacher and she was known throughout the state. 'cause she, she taught the, uh, advanced math classes, you know, the geometry, the trigonometry and all of that. But she had what they called the Euclidean Math Club. And I mean, yes. You know, so it's</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a146" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts290" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts291">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Fancy. And we had Spanish clubs, you know, French clubs. Oh, cool. We had, we didn't have Interact. And some of the newer names, uh, newer clubs they have now, we had student government. 'cause I remember I was vice president of our class. Wow. And, uh, but we had many, we had band, we had a very good band. 'cause we had some really good band directors over the years. And we had, um, we had a civil air patrol unit. I'll never forget that, because that was through the Air Force. Wow. And that was a, an organization that they put in schools. And we ended up having one of the largest in the state. 'cause we had over a hundred kids were in the civil air patrol. So it was just that distinction of putting on your uniform Yeah. And going to practice. And then co uh, competing around the states. Oh</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a148" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts294" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts295">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Really? Mm-hmm. It was Silver Air Patrol. And I remember the, uh, you know, the, the, the, uh, young men from the Air Force would come in and they're the ones that started it on school campuses, high school campuses. 'cause I had not heard of it either until then. And since it was something new and they looked good, you know, my, I asked my parents if I could be a member of it. And so I was able to do that. And I was in the band. 'cause I, I, I did the marching band, uh, stage band and jazz band. I played the flute.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a150" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts298" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts299">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I felt like, and to me, nowadays I look at flute is just so romantic. &lt;laugh&gt;. It is. It is that flute music.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a152" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts302" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts303">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Too beautiful.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a154" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts306" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts307">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Wow. See, and I, and I, I had my flute until, wow. It was probably, I got rid of my flute, I want to say in the, in the early eighties. I finally got rid of. But I had it for a while. Yes. I just, I thought one day I'd do it again, but I never really did. And I said, well, you know, some kid, 'cause I donated it to a school because I felt like, you know, some kid could use it. It's just sitting. Yeah. It was one of those momentums. But I said, you know, it could be better used if someone else had it. Yeah, of course. But I did enjoy that. We had home at clubs. I mean, we had all kinds of clubs. Really? Yeah. That's awesome. And they were very, they were very active.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a156" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts310" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts311">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh, yes. That's awesome. Because I'm, I'm still very good friends. I had a girlfriend, uh, that we went from elementary through college together. And I remember going to through Cook with every, we were the only two gray hair, uh, freshmen in the class. And everybody thought because she was from Palka, you know, Putnam County, they thought we were actually sisters &lt;laugh&gt;. And we laugh about it now. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a158" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts314" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts315">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>You know, I told 'em, I said, I, I've been gray most of my life. I said I started turning gray at seven.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a160" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts318" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts319">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so I'm just used to it. And I, I tried dye it a couple of times, said, that's just too messy. I'm not dealing with that. You know, years ago, you know, and I, they may have had it, but I just remember the coloring getting on things. You</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a162" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts322" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts323">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Of times. Right. And I, and it is, no, that's not worth it. If it was that bad, God wouldn't have given it to me. &lt;laugh&gt; that &lt;laugh&gt;.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a163" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts324" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts325">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And I just moved on. But I, I did, I did it a couple of times. I just like wearing a wig and I can't imagine wearing one for years. Like I know some people do. Yeah. But I remember, I thought, I went through that phase, and I'll never forget, I was sitting in class in college, I had this wig on. I had my hair braided underneath, and I just raised my hand. And back then the, the, some of the professors would actually seat you by your name, you know your last name. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. So, so most of the time I was on the front row, I just raised my hand &lt;laugh&gt;. And I told her, I said, excuse me, but I have got to take this off</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a165" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts328" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts329">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And I just reached up and pulled it off, put it in my purse. My classmates, they said they could not ever, they couldn't even imagine &lt;laugh&gt;. Oh my God. I said, well, it was neatly braided. I said, but I just couldn't handle that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a167" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts332" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts333">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I totally understand. So I did that. Understand.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a169" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts336" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts337">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And We taught it all. I mean, they taught it all. I remember, um, a couple of my history teachers, and we went through this a a lot of the basic things, but we did, they spent, uh, they uh, they integrated a lot of black history because it wasn't in the textbooks. Okay. Yeah. And so what they did, knowing the history, they embedded that into whatever they were doing. So they also met state and district requirements, but they enhanced on that. Yeah. And that was most appreciative because, uh, we used to do all kinds of, uh, they, back then they called 'em assemblies, assembly programs and, you know, portraying various, uh, uh, inventors, musicians, whatever. And it was just always embedded. They, they did it on their own. They embedded the history as they found out about it into our classes. Yeah. So that we would really know and know our own people because sometimes it was just people right here in our community. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a171" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts340" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts341">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. And because, and I tell people it's not real US history. When you eliminate a lot of things that we've learned over the years, why is it that our history is left out mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. And the more, you know, even when I do Bible study or whatever, and the more I learn, I say, well, you know, there's a, a thing of jealousy or some people have to suppress someone else so that they can rise to the top.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a173" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts344" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts345">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>System. Right. And that's what, you know, that's what I say, you know about, to me, even with this, they call it the critical race theory. Yeah. And I said a lot of that is about slavery. Why would you think that those are just stories? Because I had be being that my father, like I said, was born and raised here, and he was the baby of his family. Okay. And there were, I think six of them. Wow. So, and if he's the baby, and he was born in 1902, and I was always interested in history myself. And I would go around and talk to my elders just to get that like you all are doing. But I didn't, it wasn't formalized. Yeah. That oral history. And my aunt told me a story about what now we call the Maltby oak tree down on the courthouse lawn. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. And she said back in the day, that was known as the hanging tree.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a175" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts348" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts349">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. And she said that she have seen people hang there for more than one day. Wow. You know, because it was to teach a lesson to the other blacks, you know, and, and years ago, and I think they still may call them cinder blocks. And I remember my dad telling me some stories, but I always backed it up. He never knew that I would go and talk to his sisters. 'cause he was the youngest and the only boy. But, uh, I would talk to his sisters and they would tell me the same thing. And they didn't know that I'm talking to both of them. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. You know. And so I said, well, you know, that is, you know, that's really, you know, 'cause as a young person, you know, you're not always think that some of these things people are seeing is always, you know, it's not a whole lot of truth in them.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a176" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts350" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts351">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But, you know, they would tell me about these people being, and mostly men where they would tie their legs to what they call cinder blocks. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. And drop 'em off the bridge. And my aunt told me, she says, if they ever dredge that bridge, I don't know how many cinder blocks they would find, but there's got to be a lot of 'em. She said, because she remember a lot of people that actually, wow. You know, she's deceased now too. But you know, that, uh, were just dropped over for sometimes simple, just what we would think of as simple things. Yeah. But you know, a lot of people were either dropped over the ridge or they were hung on the lawn. Because my father, I'll never forget, he told us, he said, I don't spank. He says, but now one time you'll get a spanking if I ever catch you congregating under that tree, because that's nothing to be proud of.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a177" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts352" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts353">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. But his sister told me, which, and that, and that truly became a real story. She said, God, don't like ugly. She says, but that tree that they're trying to make so historical, she didn't use the term historical. She said, but she says, I'm gonna tell you, she said, God is going to kill that tree from the inside out. And when they find out about it, it will be dying on the inside. And that's exactly what happened. And I remember them, you know, they spent tens of thousands of dollars trying to preserve it. But she told me that story. And it was, I, I just couldn't believe it. But she said that tree, she said they, you know, there was a Dr. Maltby, I wanna say he was a veterinarian, but she told me that story.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a179" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts356" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts357">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh yes. You know, and you know, they'll, you know, try to lift up the limbs. 'cause it was a, it's a beautiful tree. Yeah. But for the purpose that it was used years ago, she said, that's why God was just gonna kill it. She said, they'll, it, it, it just won't stand on its own.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a181" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts360" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts361">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Sometimes there was, uh, and I remember the Ku Klux Klan was really, really, you know, uh,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a182" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts362" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts363">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Present big around? It was, you know, was they Yes. Because I remember, you know, sometimes after faculty meetings, you know, we would stay with my mom and sometimes it would be almost near dark when we'd be headed home. And once we turned off the highway to get to our house, you know, there would be men in their hoods standing trying to stop the car. But she &lt;laugh&gt; she was a smart lady. Frightening. Yes. And she would just tell us to put our heads in our laps, and she would put her feet on that accelerator. My mother's</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a184" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts366" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts367">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And they would just fall into the bushes. And I remember, you know, sometimes at night, um, it happened two or three times. I know. But when you turn the lights out at night to go to bed, all of a sudden you see all of this light outside and it would be crosses burning in the front yard.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a186" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts370" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts371">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes. And you know, where the separation, as far as, you know, blacks and whites and water fountains downtown, I'll never forget we had a water fountain down in front of the, um, the courthouse. One said white one said black, but the black one was always about a foot or two lower than seemed like it, you know. Yeah. On the other. But, uh, as far as the school itself, oh. And I remember not being able to even try on clothes. Your parents go to buy clothes. You, you couldn't put 'em on. And I remember, you know, the clerk said, well, blacks can't try on clothes because they would taint them and then they can't sell them.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a188" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts374" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts375">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But Right. But my mom then, you know, she went on, she, she wouldn't buy any, she just went on home and made 'em.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a190" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts378" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts379">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But, you know, as far as, but I'm trying to think. We were just so closely knit though at the school till we actually, I think, protected each other. You know, we would have negative things said, but I don't remember anything physical ever happening on campus whenever we were there. You know, things would happen in the neighborhood either before or after school in the evening on the weekends. But, you know, being threatened that happened.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a192" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts382" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts383">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Uh, sometimes yes. That's like the crosses being burned or I remember,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a193" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts384" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts385">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>You know, um, when they did, when, when, when integration was enforced. I do remember the, and our principal was one of the lead principals in the state, because Central Academy was the first black accredited high school in the state of Florida. And he was just so well known throughout the state. And when they forced integration, one of the saddest things I'll never forget, because it just broke everybody's heart, is that he became, they, they wouldn't give him a principalship. Not even leave him there where he was as principal. But they, uh, but the district moved him to an assistant principal position and his job was textbooks. And the saddest thing was to see him on this school campus every day, pushing a grocery cart, &lt;affirmative&gt;, checking the textbooks, you in the classroom, making sure teachers had, you know, whatever. But I mean,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a195" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts388" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts389">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I want to say, I'm trying to think after Mr. Brooks. I think initially it did, and then they did get in a, a, a black principal. But it was just that initially I think it was more of we are gonna show them.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a197" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts392" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts393">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And then even me, myself, I picked up the phone one day. I was assistant principal at Palka High &lt;laugh&gt;, and I'm overscheduling and hiring of teachers and I, and this was in the eighties. I remember picking up the phone one day and this person, because my thing is, if you're in a business, the phone should not have to ring over three times. And I heard that third ring. It wasn't picked up and it rang Well, I picked it up instantly, you know, and I just, all I could say was, hello. And this person just went on and on about Miss Asia and how, why would she have that kind of job is being over scheduling when you know that blacks uh, don't, don't have the ability to think analytically. I mean, and it went on and on and on. And this was a very respected person in the community. &lt;laugh&gt;, oh</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a199" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts396" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts397">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh. And I just let him go on. But my blood pressure, I'm sure if they had checked my stroke level right then I'd have been right there next to having, having a stroke. Oh, you have to have a lot of control. I went on and, you know, and I, I'm listening and when he did take a deep breath, 'cause he was really telling me all about myself and the lack of intellect that I have and I mean, all of this. And so when he did finally catch your breath, I said, well, let me just help you. He, I said, if you can be at the school within the hour, I will make sure that we sit with Miss Asia and get all of this straightened out. Because he was, and it was all about a schedule change for his daughter. And it's like, this is</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a201" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts400" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts401">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But no, he, oh my, oh yes. He had to just tell me in know, uncertain terms that I was not even intelligent enough to do that job. And</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a203" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts404" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts405">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>This was back in the eighties.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a205" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts408" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts409">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. But it was after I left Central Academy. But just an experience that I had and I'm thinking, this makes no sense. And so I went to the principal, I told him what had happened and I said, but I want you to stay in your office 'cause I'm gonna show you all 1 0 1 and how an intelligent black woman can tell this executive without being ugly to him. Mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. And I'm the only person in the school that was doing it. And the principal had never done it in his life. &lt;laugh&gt; neither had the superintendent. And he says, oh lord, Cynthia. I said, no, you just stay there. I went to his secretary, told her what was happening. I said, he's coming. And when he comes, don't call me. I just want you to, uh, beat me twice. Hang up and beat me again. I'll know it's him.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a206" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts410" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts411">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And bless her heart, she said she thought that he was actually gonna have a heart attack, &lt;laugh&gt;. 'cause I just walked out and introduced myself. But I did, I told him, and he, I had this really grip on me, &lt;laugh&gt;. I told him, I said, well, you did not allow me to even introduce myself before you started, you know, telling me what I was not capable of doing. And I just want you to know that the superintendent's never done scheduling. Neither had the principal. So I'm it. I said, and I can definitely help you. But I told him, you know how these, this is what his child selected. You and your wife both signed it and I keep 'em for the year. I said, so why don't you come on back to my office? And he was, he turned white as a sheep. I mean, he was, he didn't speak to me. I know for about it seemed like a couple of years before he could, he could even look me in the face. I mean, he should</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a208" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts414" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts415">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But yeah. But my experience though, back to Central Academy, I just, I totally enjoyed it. And I still, like I said, uh, what my graduating class, we still gather every other month except during the pandemic, you know, before the pandemic. And we would, we'd just have dinner, no meeting. We'd go out to dinner, uh, at Corky Bells that says, that's where everybody want to go. And we just have fun. And we still do till today.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a210" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts418" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts419">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh yes.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a212" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts422" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts423">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes. Ministers, you know, your had your minister and then your teachers, you on that totem pole, &lt;laugh&gt;, they came right under, right under ministers because they had a large influence. Yeah. But, uh, I mean, you became above the teachers. But, uh, and they were usually the, um, the leaders in the community, you know, people gather at the churches. And the churches would truly support the teacher. I mean, support the parents as well as the students. And they supported the teachers too. But sometimes they would be a spokesperson for the parents who didn't feel comfortable coming into the schools.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a214" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts426" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts427">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, the pillars of the community. Yes.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a216" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts430" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts431">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I think they wanted it to happen, but being uncertain or fearful on what was it going to take and what were they going to give up? Because they wanted equality. Of course. Yeah. But they were just not given equality because of the separation. And some people still fight it today, you know, that's the sad part about it. But they wanted it, they wanted to be able to have the new textbooks. They wanted funding to be able to do a lot of the things, you know, uh, that other schools, uh, were doing, but not always granted permission to do that. Yeah. You know, even something as simple as a school board, I mean, that's not gonna affect anybody in the classroom, but it would just make life simpler. And the school is supposed to be, you know, that bridgeway between, you know, uh, you know, students and parents, you know, where you can get out and just enjoy yourself, but yeah. Was not always possible.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a218" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts434" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts435">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I started out in fourth grade because now when I was at Cookman though, I was selected to go to New York to help segregate school district. And right outside of Rochester, it was the Genesee Valley School District. And there were no blacks in the community, no blacks in the schools. But I had to agree to go and do that. You know, I mean, I didn't have to, but I was asked mm-hmm &lt;affirmative&gt;. Knowing that my grades for that semester would come from the University of Rochester. That was one of the scariest parts for a lot of people because the, uh, the president said, if I agree to do that, then I could pick, because my advisor said, well, she's pretty outgoing, so I think she can do it. And I'll never forget, I got this phone call about nine 30 at night from the school. It's like, what is this? &lt;laugh&gt;?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a219" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts436" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts437">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so you, we did that. So I went there, yes. And still, and a white family, you were gonna have to live with the white family. Oh. And so, but it worked out perfectly over 50 years. And we still communicate today really the family that I lived with. So you had a good experience. Very good experience. And I brought a lot of those experiences back to my classroom and my career. So after that, where did you head to? And then I, uh, I did four. I was hired as a fourth grade teacher here in Putnam. And then while I, okay. I taught fourth grade for six years, and then I was, then they brought in the reading lab, federal funded, they called it chapter one back in the day. And it was a program to help children learn to read. And so I became a, a reading teacher.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a220" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts438" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts439">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Uh, and then I was recruited to Palka High School to become a guidance counselor because they did not have a black guidance counselor at the high school. And one of the things of opening that school was that they would always have a black guidance counselor so that, you know, there would be someone to connect with. And I guess they must have recruited me for two or three years. And I finally said, yes, &lt;laugh&gt;. But at the same time, I had already gotten my master's degree because having been to New York and saw the things that these kids had, I mean, calculators were built into the desk. I never seen that. I haven't even seen that. Wow. This was back in the seventies. Wow. That must have been, it was a very exclusive school, uh, school district. And so, uh, I didn't have ever gotten, had that opportunity, but a lot of the opportunities that the kids had in that district, I was able to implement as a teacher and, and even more as a principal.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a221" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts440" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts441">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And, uh, so I did the guidance counselor for several years at the high school. Then I became the academic administrator. I did the scheduling and hiring of the principal, I mean the teachers. And then I became coordinator of elementary ed. We had a director, and I became coordinator. So I was over special programs for the elementary, uh, students. And after that I went to the, oh, I went to the district office as coordinator. And then one day, all of a sudden it clicked. We do not have a black principal in Putnam County. So I got up, didn't even tell my director &lt;laugh&gt;, who was right there in the same office. I went down to the superintendent's office. And at the time we had a female, white female. And uh, I told her, I said, Jerry, 'cause we, because I used to be one of her supervisors at the high school, but she became superintendent.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a222" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts442" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts443">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>'cause we have an elected superintendent. And &lt;laugh&gt;. I said, ger, I said, Jerry, I said, I am going to have to do something. I'm, but I want you to know that I will be applying for principalship in whatever school that I feel comfortable with. But, but, and it has nothing to do with you nor my position. I said, but I owe it to my community to give them that opportunity to have someone in that particular position. And I did. I ended up, you know, getting the, getting a job and went on out and became principal. Started out in Crescent City, the elementary school down in Crescent City. At the time, it was Crescent City Elementary. Now it's Middleton Burn Elementary. And then by the end of that first year, she moved me to Palka &lt;laugh&gt;, you know, because she was having concerns with some of the, with, with some principals. And so she wanted me to come to Palka, of course. I said yes.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a223" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts444" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts445">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I went, uh, to James A. Long elementary. I stayed there for about almost three years. And then David Buckles became superintendent 'cause he and I were principals together. And then he became superintendent and he wanted me to come back downtown,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a225" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts448" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts449">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>All over the place. And so I became director of elementary ed.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a227" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts452" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts453">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And then, uh, from director of elementary ed, uh, to, I mean, yeah, I went back down there with him as director of elementary ed. And then I was promoted to area director of instruction for the Palatka area. They had three at the time. Wow. You had one for South Putnam, one for West Putnam, and then one for the Palatka area. But at the time, they didn't want to increase my salary. No, that's not going to work.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a229" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts456" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts457">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I said, you have one white male in South Putnam, one in West Putnam together. I have more schools than the two of them together. No,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a231" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts460" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts461">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>More. And that's exactly what I said. And I said, and salaries are public, and I will be checking them on a regular basis.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a233" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts464" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts465">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>They didn't pay me more, but I paid. I said, I'm not gonna make a penny less.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a235" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts468" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts469">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>You know, because they said, well, in two or three years, are you kidding me,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a237" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts472" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts473">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I said, this is the 21st century and you have to pay for black magic &lt;laugh&gt;. And that's really what I told him. I said, no, you can. You're not gonna do this. You're not gonna, yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a239" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts476" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts477">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So you want me to correct a lot of the wrongs that had been done?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a241" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts480" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts481">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right? Yeah. I said, and I have twice as many schools as the rest of 'em. I had what, nine schools and one had three or four, you know, it's like, no, this is, this is just not even fair. That's a lot. Yeah. Right. And it was elementary through high.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a243" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts484" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts485">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh, no.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a245" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts488" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts489">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Before hired? Yes. I retired as area, as area, uh, director. And then in 2012 I ran for superintendent of schools.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a247" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts492" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts493">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>It, we, I don't think we ever got to that level. And which was sad, you know, because you had some, some situations were fighting the others, you know, as far as what to offer and those kinds of things. And then giving teachers flexibility, principals flexibility. You know, if some people enjoy micromanaging, and I'm not a micromanager. I want to, to, to, to allow professionals to be a professional. Because even as a principal, my teachers and I told them, and they thought that was kind of different. That they weren't required to be at faculty meetings, but they were responsible to implement whatever decisions we made. I hardly ever had anybody not there. And if they, if they weren't there, and if they were on campus and not there, then they should have a buddy to cover for them because they need to know the information.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a249" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts496" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts497">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>At James A. Long James A. Long, yeah. Okay.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a251" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts500" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts501">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>It's progressed? Some, but there's still a divide that that gap is still there because it's not embraced, I don't think on both sides the way that it really should be. And I think we just don't talk enough about it. You know, to me, if there's a concern, you put it on the table, you address it. And a lot of decisions are made without having both sides at the table. Yeah. And I really believe what Shirley Chisholm said years ago, you know, if, if they don't offer you a seat at the table, just bring a lawn chair, &lt;laugh&gt;, you gotta be there. You just gotta be there.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a253" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts504" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts505">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Palka High is the only high school in the Palatka area. Area. Yeah. And you have Central aca, I mean, central Academy &lt;laugh&gt;, you have Crescent City Junior Senior High, then you've got Interlachen, and now you have Qi Roberts, because that's a special school. Mm.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a255" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts508" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts509">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>It's still public school, but you know, they offer the Cambridge program and things of that nature. And then you have, uh, you have, um, yeah, there's a, a charter school. You have some charter schools around, but, and charter schools are public schools 'cause they're public funded and a lot of people's, they, they have more flexibility. Yeah. You know, the, the law is such that it's not the same.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a257" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts512" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts513">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>In the, that they came in my office? Yeah. Periodically, but not that often. Um, because by then when I went to Palka High, you know, it's been,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a258" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts514" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts515">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>It was, let see, Palka High open, I want say in 6 76. And so it's been, it had been a while when they integrated the, the both schools. And that's how Palka High got, so they could put 'em all in one school. Yeah. And, uh, there were tensions periodically, but I didn't have to be a part of most of that every now and then. And when you had a principal, I remember Dr. Shiver, he was actually, he's from, he was from Florida, but his outlook on life was totally different and equality and justice for all. Which I did like that because he and I, we had very good dialogue. And he actually, I remember him telling the custodial staff is that if they ever run out of the, the paint colors of that school, then they were gonna have to sit down with him because he didn't want, because things happen.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a259" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts516" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts517">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But he tried to nip it in the bud instantly. People would come and spray paint ugly things, you know, on the building. Uh, we had some students that were, I call it youth members of the KKK, they would advertise in the schools. And one, one of our jobs as administrators was we had to walk, everybody had their own designated area. So before the kids came, you know, we had to walk our area. And if you see any kind of posters or whatever up, you had to get 'em gone before the, the faculty and the kids came to school.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a260" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts518" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts519">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Crazy. But, you know, but things like that do happen. Yeah. And I think that if you just leave it to the younger people, things will be a whole lot better I think when it comes to Yeah. You know, integration. But the older, you know, the, the, the more the adults are the ones that keep that going. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a262" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts522" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts523">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. Um, well, yeah. There was resistance there, you know, and some people, some people didn't even want me to be a principal because I was black. I had some people, you know, that would want to take their kids out of the school.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a264" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts526" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts527">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But that's your choice.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a266" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts530" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts531">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'm here &lt;laugh&gt; and I'm gonna treat your child just as though that child was my child. And I don't have any kids. But you know, I just feel like, you know, we all humans, we all bleed red blood. I don't bleed black blood and you read bleed white blood, we all bleed, bleed red blood. And you know, that's just the way God made us. And we make a choice not to embrace each other and each other differences. And just, to me, my thought, I've always felt like when you know better, you do better. But also everybody has something positive to contribute. It may not be the same, but when we put it all together, what a wonderful world it could be.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a268" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts534" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts535">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>And I'm sure is my time way over. Yeah, no,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a271" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts540" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts541">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I would say when I went to Hawaii. Wow. I mean that's not even out of the country, but I really did enjoy Hawaii and I actually, I really did enjoy Korea. I really did enjoy, the culture is totally different, but the people were really, you know, they were so nice. Really. And what, and I guess I was impressed too because you know, that's in Asia. My last name is Asia &lt;laugh&gt; and I had a very positive experience because they couldn't believe that Asia was in Asia and I never had to pay for, uh, food. I never had to pay for transportation.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a273" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts544" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts545">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh my god. You know, all of these things were, it is like, wow, I can't believe that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a275" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts548" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts549">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well my nephew was stationed there in the Air Force.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a277" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts552" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts553">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. Wherever he was stationed in the world. I just went to visit &lt;laugh&gt;.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a280" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts558" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts559">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>More reason and when I went to shopping was really good when I got there, that's my dollar was worth $3. And from 12:00 AM to 6:00 AM your doll, your dollar doubles. So my dollar. Oh yes. Wow. And, and the stores and everything was still open all night. I'm sure you it was the best.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a282" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts562" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts563">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Every, anything. My nephew said, if you can think of it and explain it, they can make it</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a284" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts566" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts567">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Or bring</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a286" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts570" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts571">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Period. Yeah. And, and bring, and bring pictures with you. Wow. Just so that you could show them what you want.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a288" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts574" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts575">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>We, and a lot of people think that a lot of the things that you get are fake, but, and they even taught me how to find out what the one guy told me in one of the stories, he said, miss Asia, he said, always keep you a cigarette lighter. &lt;laugh&gt;. He said Plastic will crumble instantly. &lt;laugh&gt;.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a290" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts578" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts579">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But yes, I mean it was just, to me it's just wonderful and it's just, it, like I said, it was different, but it was just a real, real good experience. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a292" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts582" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts583">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Plus his, my nephew's mother and I, we traveled together.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a294" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts586" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts587">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. And I like, I like things that's different. I'll, I'll try it. You know, if I don't like it, I don't have to go back. Yeah. You know,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a296" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts590" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts591">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. And so, hey, why not you only go this way once they didn't pros they didn't promise us a second time around. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a298" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts594" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts595">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Oh yes. I enjoyed it. I hope I, you know,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a300" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts598" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts599">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>&lt;laugh&gt;. Oh, okay. Okay. I was waiting on Rebecca to stop us and tell us we had gone over.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		</TIER>
